<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<section xml:id="sec_8-1_Integrate_by_Substitution" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Section 8.1: Integration by Substitution</title>

  <slide>
    <title>Integration by Substitution</title>
    
    <p>
      One of the most common issues with finding antiderivatives deals with undoing the chain rule.
      We saw previously a pattern when dealing with integrals like:
      <md>
        \int e^{3x} \,dx \qquad \int \sin(2x) \, dx
      </md>
      that we needed to <em>divide</em> by the <m>u'</m> in combination with simple antiderivatives.
      <md>
        <mrow> \int e^{3x} \, dx \amp = \frac{1}{3} e^{3x} + C </mrow>
        <mrow> \int \sin(2x) \, dx \amp = -\frac{1}{2} \cos(2x) + C </mrow>
      </md>
      But this strategy really only worked because <m>u'</m> happened to be a constant.
    </p>
    <p>
      The generalization of this strategy is required if <m>u'</m> is a formula and is called
      <term>integration by substitution</term>.
    </p>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Recognizing the Chain Rule</title>
    
    <p>
      Computing integrals involving <term>compositions</term> requires first being proficient in calculating derivatives involving the <term>chain rule</term>.
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\Big[ e^{x^2} \Big]</mrow>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\Big[ \ln(x^2+4) \Big]</mrow>
      </md>
      The derivative of <m>F(u)</m> will always be a <term>product</term> <m>F'(u) \cdot u'</m>.
    </p>
  </slide>
  <slide>
    <title>Steps of the Method of Substitution</title>
    
    <p>
      Integration by substitution is a tool to <em>recognize</em> and <em>simplify</em> this multiplication.
      Identify in the integrand the presence of a composition <m>f(u)</m> with a function <m>u</m>.
      This function normally should be <em>multiplied</em> by a constant multiple of <m>u'</m>.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Identify <m>u</m> and calculate <m>u'</m>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Substitute the formula <m>u</m> by the symbol <m>u</m> (or something else convenient).</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use <m>du = u' \cdot dx</m> to substitute the differential <m>dx</m> by the formula <m>\frac{1}{u'} \cdot du</m>.
            Ideally this will cancel any remaining formulas.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>This transforms the integral to a new variable with a simpler formula. Find the antiderivative and back-substitute.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Examples</title>
    
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p><m>\displaystyle \int xe^{-x^2} \, dx </m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\displaystyle \int (2x+4)^8 \, dx </m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\displaystyle \int \frac{4t}{(t^2+3)^7} \, dt </m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\displaystyle \int \frac{w^2}{w^3+1} \, dw </m></p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Definite Integrals with Substitution</title>
    
    <p>
      Although it is possible to find an antiderivative using substitution and then evaluate the change,
      it is sometimes faster or beneficial to rewrite the definite integral in the new variable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Suppose that an indefinite integral using substitution would transform
      <md>
        \int f(x) \,dx = \int g(u) \, du
      </md>
      Then a definite integral can similarly be transformed
      <md>
        \int_a^b f(x) \,dx = \int_{u(a)}^{u(b)} g(u) \, du
      </md>
      It is essential that you maintain the order of the limits of integration, even if they seem to have been reversed.
    </p>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Examples of Definite Integrals Using Substitution</title>
    
    <p>
      Rewrite and then evaluate the definite integrals using substitution:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>\displaystyle \int_1^3 4x(x^2-1)^2 \, dx</m>, <m>u=x^2-1</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\displaystyle \int_0^1 x \sqrt{1-x} \, dx</m>, <m>u=1-x</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </slide>
</section>
